<h2 data-start="1443" data-end="1507">🌐 Part 2: <strong data-start="1457" data-end="1507">Can it access the web to fetch real-time info?</strong></h2>
<blockquote data-start="1509" data-end="1614">
<p data-start="1511" data-end="1614"><em data-start="1511" data-end="1614">“Would it need to access the World Wide Web to pull info for correlation or updating HTML responses?”</em></p>
</blockquote>
<h3 data-start="1616" data-end="1648">✅ Yes — but with conditions:</h3>
<p data-start="1650" data-end="1717">JavaScript (in the browser) can do <strong data-start="1685" data-end="1712">limited external access</strong> via:</p>
<ul data-start="1719" data-end="1850">
<li data-start="1719" data-end="1760">
<p data-start="1721" data-end="1760"><code data-start="1721" data-end="1730">fetch()</code> API calls (GET/POST requests)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1761" data-end="1806">
<p data-start="1763" data-end="1806">Calling third-party APIs (public or custom)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1807" data-end="1850">
<p data-start="1809" data-end="1850">Web scraping (limited &amp; needs CORS proxy)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1852" data-end="1856">BUT:</p>
<ul data-start="1857" data-end="2018">
<li data-start="1857" data-end="1941">
<p data-start="1859" data-end="1941">It’s sandboxed. You’d need a <strong data-start="1888" data-end="1913">CORS-enabled endpoint</strong> or a <strong data-start="1919" data-end="1941">server-side bridge</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1942" data-end="2018">
<p data-start="1944" data-end="2018">Some sites block direct fetch calls unless they're meant to be public APIs</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="2020" data-end="2037">🧠 Best move:</h3>
<ul data-start="2038" data-end="2255">
<li data-start="2038" data-end="2114">
<p data-start="2040" data-end="2114"><strong data-start="2040" data-end="2081">Build your own server-side API mirror</strong> (Node.js/Express or n8n webhook)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2115" data-end="2202">
<p data-start="2117" data-end="2202">JS frontend sends query → server fetches real-time web info → sends back trimmed data</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2203" data-end="2255">
<p data-start="2205" data-end="2255">Response displayed along with your correlation map</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2257" data-end="2469"><strong data-start="2257" data-end="2263">Or</strong>, ask DeepSight to build an "information-gathering assistant" module that accepts a topic and fetches summaries via DuckDuckGo, Wikipedia, or PubMed APIs. Then plug that into your recursive response engine.</p>
